\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Antiperiodic spatial Dirac modes}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Keep the [spatial Clifford module](fdip-000R)
#{S=(\mathbb C^2)^{\otimes n}}, with #{\dim_{\mathbb C}S=2^n},
and periods #{N_j\ge3}. Give scalar fields on the fundamental box
#{0\le x_j<N_j} the antiperiodic extension
##{f(x+N_je_j)=-f(x)\qquad(1\le j\le n).}
The forward shift #{T_j^-} uses this extension at a seam. It is a
signed permutation, hence unitary in the counting pairing; shifts in
different coordinates commute. They act on the lattice factor only,
so they commute with the represented generators on #{S}. Define
##{D^- =\sum_{j=1}^n\rho(\iota(\varepsilon_j))\otimes
          \frac{T_j^--(T_j^-)^{-1}}2.}
These are fixed flat line twists, each with holonomy minus one.}

\p{For labels #{0\le r_j<N_j}, set
##{p_j=\frac{(2r_j+1)\pi}{N_j},\qquad
\chi_r(x)=\left(\prod_jN_j\right)^{-1/2}
 \ue^{\ui\sum_jp_jx_j}.}
The exponential obeys the declared antiperiodic extension. Differences
of two allowed momenta are periodic Fourier momenta, so the same
geometric-sum proof as \ref{fdip-000S} makes these functions a complete
orthonormal basis of fields on the box. On its momentum block,
##{\begin{aligned}
D^-(p)&=\ui\sum_j\rho(\iota(\varepsilon_j))\sin p_j,\\
-D^-(p)^2&=\left(\sum_j\sin^2p_j\right)\bbI_S.
\end{aligned}}
The square follows from the Clifford anticommutators and commuting
coordinate shifts, exactly as in the untwisted calculation.}

\p{A block is zero precisely when every #{\sin p_j=0}; otherwise its
inverse is #{-D^-(p)/(\sum_j\sin^2p_j)}. Since #{0<p_j<2\pi}, the
only possible zero is #{p_j=\pi}, which requires
#{2r_j+1=N_j}. It occurs once when #{N_j} is odd and never when it is
even. Hence
##{\dim_{\mathbb C}\ker D^-=
\begin{cases}
2^n,&\text{all periods are odd},\\
0,&\text{at least one period is even}.
\end{cases}}
For two directions the comparison with \ref{fdip-000T} is
##{\begin{array}{c|ccc}
(N_1,N_2)&(4,4)&(4,3)&(3,3)\\\hline
\dim\ker D&16&8&4\\
\dim\ker D^-&0&0&4
\end{array}}
These are dimensions in the specified reducible module, not physical
species or chirality counts.}

\p{The twist changes exact zeros without removing the centered
symbol's extra low-energy branches. For large #{N_j}, an allowed
momentum lies within #{\pi /N_j} of each corner coordinate #{0} or
#{\pi}, with distance to #{0} understood modulo #{2\pi}. Thus
#{|\sin p_j|\le\pi /N_j} near every such corner. After restoring a
fixed-length mesh spacing #{a_j=L_j/N_j}, the corresponding
#{|\sin p_j|/a_j} is bounded by #{\pi /L_j}; branches near the
#{\pi} corners remain at finite energy alongside the branch near zero.
This does not say that the rescaled antiperiodic eigenvalues tend to
zero at fixed circle lengths. The additional branches are the
centered-difference doubling problem, whose sine symbol is discussed
in \citet{Section 9.1, Equations (9.6)–(9.7), p. 20}{wingate2025lattice}.
A boundary twist alone does not supply its Wilson-operator remedy.}
